speechtext:
I know what the hon. member for Centre Toronto (Mr. Bristol) is going to say. I can divine his ques-109}
tion. I am well aware that the . Haggerty does not go to Montreal, but she was able to make that amount of money carrying twenty odd loads from Port Arthur and Fort William to Port Colborne, and when you consider the fact that in the United States ships of that kind are compelled to conform to certain rates on inland waters as well as on the coast, I think you will concede that the Haggerty has taken advantage of her position, and has availed herself of the privileges which the coastal regulations grant her. At five cents you can see what she has been able to accomplish. Take the Grant Morden, which is capable of carrying
500,000	bushels of wheat or 740,000 bushels of oats, and when you consider that she makes some twenty trips in a season from Port Arthur and Fort William to Port Colborne, you can understand what an enormous income must be derived from that source at the rates I have stated.

Like the preceding speaker, Sir, I simply rise to present a petition from another of the shipping points much interested in this Bill-the city of Port Arthur. It reads as follows:
March 9, 1920.
Memorial Protesting against the Placing of Inland Shipping under the Board of Railway Commissioners.
Whereas the Board of Trade of Port Arthur has been advised that notice has been given by J. E. Armstrong, tile honourable member for Lambton, to again bring before the Dominion Parliament his many-times rejected Bill for the controlling of Canadian water-borne traffic;
And whereas it must be apparent to all that such control will be detrimental to the industries and the people of Canada and is apparently sought for in the interests of the railroads of the United States and Canada by the outstanding fact that vessels to subsist and develop must necessarily charge a greatly lowered rate on the carrying of freight than the railways, otherwise a lower tariff;
And whereas, Canadian-borne freight both east and westbound on the Great Lakes is more or less sporadic, with periods of shipping activity and, at other times, business so lacking as to compel the tying up of vessels;
And whereas, under such conditions no set rates of tariff can be adjusted with any fairness to vessels, and that all ratings must necessarily be set according to the exigencies of supply and demand, with a lower rating absolutely guaranteed to shippers by railway competition ;
And whereas, adequate shipping both on the Great Lakes and ocean is absolutely ncessary for Canada's fullest development and that any act detrimental to such development would be nothing more or less than a national crime;
Therefore, be it resolved that the Board of Trade of the city of Port Arthur does herewith petition the Government of Canada, and the members comprising its Parliament, to once more reject the aforementioned Bill when brought before them, fully believing that such, if brought into effect, would be a menace to Canada's development both in her industries and people.
And it is further resolved that a copy of this be sent to the Premier of (Canada, the Minister of Railways and Canals, and to the members of Port Arthur and Port William, Dr. Manion, M.P., and Prank H. Keefer, K.C., M.P.
Frederick Urry,
President.
Jos. Z. Finzel,
Secretary.
I have only to say a word or two to accentuate what has been already stated in regard to the fixing of freight rates. When one considers the different sizes of ships one will realize that it is impossible to fix reasonable rates. As a parallel instance, take the fixing of the profit on the grinding of wheat into flour, at, say, twenty-five cents a barrel; that would give a 100-barrel mill only $25 a day, but it would give a 9,000-barrel mill $2,250 a day. A similar difficulty arises in attempting to fix rates applicable to large ships of 400,000 or 500,000 bushels as well as to small ships of 75,000 or 100,000 bushels. A rate that will give the small ship a fair return, will enable the big ship to make an enormous profit; while if a low rate is fixed for the big ship, the small ship will be driven off the lakes and competition will be stifled.
It was suggested that the construction of the St. Lawrence canal should be held up until some rate regulation is put into effect. As a matter of fact, it is the other way round. If the St. Lawrence canal is built it will mean a saving to the western farmers of at least six cents on every bushel of wheat, exported through obviating the breaking of bulk at Buffalo or New York. For instance, the states of Dakota and Minnesota would save $20,000,000 per annum on one product alone thereby. It is not, perhaps, realized how much more important it is to get the canal system completed than to be monkeying all the time with freight rates.
